1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,230
Okay, picture this.

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:04,910
July twenty twenty-three,
Phoenix, Arizona.

3
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:07,150
It's a hundred and ten degrees outside.

4
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:11,130
And it's been a hundred and ten or hotter
every single day for a month.

5
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:12,950
Thirty-one days in a row!

6
00:00:13,502 --> 00:00:17,732
And yet, right now, someone in Phoenix
is filling a swimming pool.

7
00:00:18,286 --> 00:00:20,476
Sprinklers are ticking on a golf course.

8
00:00:20,686 --> 00:00:24,456
There's a lake in the middle of the desert
where people paddle around in kayaks.

9
00:00:24,786 --> 00:00:27,366
And about five million people are
turning on their taps,

10
00:00:27,406 --> 00:00:29,296
and water just comes out.

11
00:00:29,721 --> 00:00:30,571
Here's the thing.

12
00:00:31,341 --> 00:00:33,551
Phoenix gets about seven
inches of rain a year.

13
00:00:33,841 --> 00:00:36,331
That's less than a third of what
falls on Los Angeles.

14
00:00:36,841 --> 00:00:39,211
This is the Sonoran Desert.
Cactus country.

15
00:00:39,801 --> 00:00:41,631
So where does the water come from?

16
00:00:42,061 --> 00:00:46,251
Your first guess might be: they pump it
from deep underground. Nope.

17
00:00:46,721 --> 00:00:51,111
For the city of Phoenix, groundwater is only
about two percent of the supply.

18
00:00:51,664 --> 00:00:55,254
Second guess: they're wasting it, and
they're about to run dry.

19
00:00:55,804 --> 00:00:57,194
Well, here's the twist.

20
00:00:57,564 --> 00:01:01,884
The average person in Phoenix actually uses
about thirty percent less water than

21
00:01:01,924 --> 00:01:03,394
they did back in nineteen ninety.

22
00:01:04,047 --> 00:01:07,337
So how does a city this big exist
in a place this dry?

23
00:01:07,987 --> 00:01:11,277
The answer is a story that starts more
than a thousand years ago,

24
00:01:11,407 --> 00:01:14,057
with people who had nothing but
sticks and baskets.

25
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:19,310
We'll meet the desert's first engineers, a
president who came to open a giant dam,

26
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:22,870
a river that travels three hundred and
thirty-six miles uphill,

27
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:26,930
and the new rules that just changed
everything. Let's go!

28
00:01:30,906 --> 00:01:36,236
Long before Phoenix, this valley belonged to a
people we now call the Hohokam.

29
00:01:36,924 --> 00:01:39,274
Starting around fourteen
hundred years ago,

30
00:01:39,524 --> 00:01:40,714
they did something amazing.

31
00:01:41,184 --> 00:01:44,434
With digging sticks, stone tools,
and baskets of dirt,

32
00:01:44,704 --> 00:01:45,774
they built canals.

33
00:01:46,224 --> 00:01:47,274
Lots of canals.

34
00:01:47,804 --> 00:01:51,174
Archaeologists estimate hundreds of
miles of main canals,

35
00:01:51,424 --> 00:01:53,634
maybe close to a thousand miles in total,

36
00:01:53,844 --> 00:01:55,254
built over many centuries.

37
00:01:55,889 --> 00:01:59,109
Some of those canals were wide enough
to drive a car down today.

38
00:01:59,149 --> 00:02:01,579
And they worked with nothing but gravity,

39
00:02:01,729 --> 00:02:06,899
a tiny slope, mile after mile, carrying
river water out to fields of corn,

40
00:02:07,209 --> 00:02:08,999
beans, squash and cotton.

41
00:02:09,645 --> 00:02:11,055
And you can still see one.

42
00:02:11,485 --> 00:02:14,975
Right next to Phoenix's main airport,
at the Pueblo Grande ruins,

43
00:02:15,325 --> 00:02:18,935
there's a park where an ancient canal bank
sits a few minutes from the runways.

44
00:02:19,445 --> 00:02:21,735
Jets taking off over
thousand-year-old plumbing.

45
00:02:22,404 --> 00:02:26,254
Then, around the fourteen hundreds,
the Hohokam world changed.

46
00:02:26,624 --> 00:02:30,014
Their big villages emptied out, and
nobody knows exactly why.

47
00:02:30,544 --> 00:02:32,414
Floods, droughts, conflict?

48
00:02:32,824 --> 00:02:33,734
Probably a mix.

49
00:02:33,904 --> 00:02:36,994
But their canals stayed, slowly
filling with sand.

50
00:02:37,587 --> 00:02:39,737
Fast forward to eighteen sixty-seven.

51
00:02:40,187 --> 00:02:44,447
A former Confederate soldier and prospector
named Jack Swilling rides through the

52
00:02:44,487 --> 00:02:47,287
valley and notices something
strange: long,

53
00:02:47,327 --> 00:02:49,737
straight ditches running
across the desert.

54
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,430
He realizes what they are. Old canals!

55
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:58,790
So he forms a company, his crew clears the old
routes and digs new ones beside them,

56
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:00,610
and soon there are farms again.

57
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:02,650
A little town grows up around them.

58
00:03:03,111 --> 00:03:05,641
A well-read friend of his, Darrell Duppa,

59
00:03:05,711 --> 00:03:06,821
suggests a name.

60
00:03:07,271 --> 00:03:10,181
A new town, rising from the
ruins of an old one,

61
00:03:10,331 --> 00:03:14,201
like the mythical bird that rises from
its own ashes. Phoenix.

62
00:03:14,844 --> 00:03:16,334
And here's my favorite part.

63
00:03:16,804 --> 00:03:21,564
Today, some of the big canals in Phoenix still
follow the routes the Hohokam laid

64
00:03:21,604 --> 00:03:22,934
out centuries ago.

65
00:03:23,364 --> 00:03:25,754
The city's plumbing is, in part, ancient.

66
00:03:28,367 --> 00:03:29,777
There was just one problem.

67
00:03:30,127 --> 00:03:33,237
The Salt River, the river that
fed all those canals,

68
00:03:33,367 --> 00:03:34,917
was completely unpredictable.

69
00:03:35,668 --> 00:03:37,458
Some years it barely trickled.

70
00:03:37,648 --> 00:03:41,198
Other years, it flooded and ripped the
canal headgates right out.

71
00:03:41,928 --> 00:03:46,084
Farmers would go from drought to disaster
in a single season. The fix?

72
00:03:46,574 --> 00:03:47,424
Store the water.

73
00:03:47,734 --> 00:03:50,844
In nineteen oh two, Congress passed
the Reclamation Act,

74
00:03:50,954 --> 00:03:53,504
a law to build big dams for the dry West.

75
00:03:53,754 --> 00:03:55,704
And local farmers jumped at it.

76
00:03:55,914 --> 00:04:00,084
They put up their own land as collateral and
formed what became the Salt River Project.

77
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:03,730
Up in the mountains, about
seventy-five miles from town,

78
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:07,270
workers stacked giant blocks of
stone into a canyon.

79
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:11,910
On March eighteenth, nineteen eleven, a
special guest showed up to dedicate it:

80
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:13,610
Theodore Roosevelt himself.

81
00:04:14,255 --> 00:04:16,145
The dam was named after him.

82
00:04:16,595 --> 00:04:20,265
Roosevelt Dam turned an unpredictable
river into a savings account.

83
00:04:20,675 --> 00:04:23,445
Fill it up in wet years, spend
it in dry ones.

84
00:04:23,999 --> 00:04:28,399
Today, the Salt River Project runs about one
hundred and thirty-one miles of main

85
00:04:28,439 --> 00:04:32,309
canals and around a thousand miles of
smaller ones across the valley.

86
00:04:32,679 --> 00:04:35,169
That's more canal than Venice, by a lot.

87
00:04:35,687 --> 00:04:37,177
And here's a fun side effect.

88
00:04:37,527 --> 00:04:41,747
So much of the river now goes into canals that
the Salt River through Phoenix is

89
00:04:41,787 --> 00:04:43,037
usually a dry bed.

90
00:04:43,427 --> 00:04:46,327
So when the city of Tempe wanted a
lake, in nineteen ninety-nine,

91
00:04:46,367 --> 00:04:51,517
they built dams across the dry riverbed and filled it
with water from somewhere else entirely.

92
00:04:52,197 --> 00:04:55,867
Which brings us to the most important
"somewhere else" in Arizona.

93
00:04:58,544 --> 00:05:02,814
The Salt and Verde rivers still supply a
big chunk of Phoenix's water.

94
00:05:03,204 --> 00:05:04,384
About fifty-eight percent.

95
00:05:04,424 --> 00:05:10,534
But the rest, about forty percent, comes from a river
that doesn't even touch the city. The Colorado.

96
00:05:11,251 --> 00:05:14,521
The Colorado River runs along
Arizona's western border,

97
00:05:14,751 --> 00:05:16,041
way out by Lake Havasu.

98
00:05:16,491 --> 00:05:19,841
For decades, Arizona had a legal
right to a big share of it,

99
00:05:19,991 --> 00:05:21,961
but no way to actually get it to Phoenix.

100
00:05:22,715 --> 00:05:25,465
One man spent most of his life
on that problem.

101
00:05:26,155 --> 00:05:29,365
Carl Hayden, Arizona's senator for
more than forty years.

102
00:05:29,775 --> 00:05:32,665
In nineteen sixty-eight, near the
very end of his career,

103
00:05:33,255 --> 00:05:36,025
Congress finally approved the
Central Arizona Project.

104
00:05:36,705 --> 00:05:39,095
Construction started in
nineteen seventy-three.

105
00:05:39,445 --> 00:05:44,245
Twenty years later, it was done: a concrete river
three hundred and thirty-six miles

106
00:05:44,285 --> 00:05:48,455
long, from Lake Havasu, through
Phoenix, all the way to Tucson.

107
00:05:48,925 --> 00:05:51,915
That's longer than the drive from
Los Angeles to Las Vegas.

108
00:05:52,516 --> 00:05:53,906
But here's the wild part.

109
00:05:54,236 --> 00:05:55,526
Water doesn't flow uphill.

110
00:05:55,936 --> 00:05:59,526
And Phoenix is higher than Lake
Havasu, Tucson even higher.

111
00:05:59,976 --> 00:06:04,346
So along the way, giant pumping plants lift
the water up again and again.

112
00:06:04,696 --> 00:06:07,066
In total, close to three thousand feet.

113
00:06:07,436 --> 00:06:11,746
That's like lifting it over two Empire State
Buildings stacked on top of each other.

114
00:06:12,236 --> 00:06:13,946
And it's not all open canal.

115
00:06:14,356 --> 00:06:18,176
In places, the water rushes through tunnels
drilled straight through desert

116
00:06:18,216 --> 00:06:21,526
mountains, and under washes,
through giant siphons.

117
00:06:21,816 --> 00:06:23,986
A whole river, hidden in plain sight.

118
00:06:24,577 --> 00:06:26,727
All that lifting takes a lot
of electricity.

119
00:06:27,297 --> 00:06:31,447
The Central Arizona Project is one of the
biggest power users in the whole state.

120
00:06:32,017 --> 00:06:34,507
Basically, Phoenix doesn't
just drink water.

121
00:06:34,817 --> 00:06:36,227
It drinks electricity, too.

122
00:06:36,864 --> 00:06:38,654
So now Phoenix had two rivers.

123
00:06:39,024 --> 00:06:40,154
Problem solved, right?

124
00:06:40,744 --> 00:06:41,934
Well, not quite.

125
00:06:44,486 --> 00:06:49,416
The Colorado River feeds about forty million
people across seven states and Mexico.

126
00:06:49,986 --> 00:06:52,636
And for more than twenty years,
it's been running low.

127
00:06:53,286 --> 00:06:56,056
Lake Mead, the giant reservoir
behind Hoover Dam,

128
00:06:56,186 --> 00:07:00,056
dropped so far that old boats started
showing up on dry land.

129
00:07:00,641 --> 00:07:04,561
In the summer of twenty twenty-two, Lake Mead
hit its lowest level since it was

130
00:07:04,601 --> 00:07:07,251
first being filled, back in the
nineteen thirties.

131
00:07:07,581 --> 00:07:11,011
That white ring around its cliffs shows
where the water used to be.

132
00:07:11,481 --> 00:07:15,331
In twenty twenty-three, the federal
government declared a deeper shortage.

133
00:07:16,121 --> 00:07:20,221
Arizona had to leave five hundred and ninety-two
thousand acre-feet of its Colorado

134
00:07:20,261 --> 00:07:21,671
River water in the lake.

135
00:07:22,081 --> 00:07:23,931
That's about a fifth of the state's share.

136
00:07:24,457 --> 00:07:29,647
An acre-foot, by the way, is enough water to
cover a football field one foot deep.

137
00:07:30,097 --> 00:07:32,627
Roughly what two or three
families use in a year.

138
00:07:32,897 --> 00:07:34,947
So we're talking about a lot of families.

139
00:07:35,554 --> 00:07:38,024
And Arizona is first in line for cuts.

140
00:07:38,474 --> 00:07:41,664
Its canal was the last one built, so
its rights are junior.

141
00:07:42,234 --> 00:07:47,054
In August twenty twenty-six, a new federal plan
set Arizona's cut at seven hundred

142
00:07:47,094 --> 00:07:51,684
and sixty thousand acre-feet a year for twenty
twenty-seven and twenty twenty-eight.

143
00:07:52,199 --> 00:07:54,129
Then there's the water under your feet.

144
00:07:54,719 --> 00:07:57,669
In June twenty twenty-three,
Arizona's governor,

145
00:07:57,799 --> 00:08:02,149
Katie Hobbs, shared the results of a new
groundwater study for the Phoenix area.

146
00:08:02,639 --> 00:08:08,109
Over the next hundred years, demand would outrun supply
by about four point eight million acre-feet.

147
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:13,490
So the state stopped approving new subdivisions that
planned to rely on groundwater alone.

148
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:17,270
Not existing homes, not cities with
their own surface water.

149
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:19,670
Just new ones banking on the aquifer.

150
00:08:20,366 --> 00:08:22,476
And this is a rule Phoenix
wrote for itself.

151
00:08:23,026 --> 00:08:26,526
Since the nineteen nineties, builders in the
Phoenix area have had to prove they

152
00:08:26,566 --> 00:08:30,476
have enough water for one hundred years before
they can sell a single new home.

153
00:08:31,111 --> 00:08:32,241
That sounds scary.

154
00:08:32,631 --> 00:08:34,081
But here's the thing about Phoenix.

155
00:08:34,331 --> 00:08:37,961
It has been planning for exactly this
moment for a long time.

156
00:08:38,291 --> 00:08:39,761
And that's where it gets clever.

157
00:08:42,336 --> 00:08:46,406
Trick number one: never waste a drop,
not even the gross ones.

158
00:08:46,856 --> 00:08:50,026
About fifty miles west of
Phoenix sits Palo Verde,

159
00:08:50,196 --> 00:08:53,186
one of the biggest nuclear power
plants in the United States.

160
00:08:53,516 --> 00:08:58,206
And it's cooled with treated wastewater from
Phoenix-area cities. Yes, sewage.

161
00:08:58,908 --> 00:09:03,198
It's believed to be the only nuclear plant in
the world that isn't next to a lake,

162
00:09:03,468 --> 00:09:04,938
a river or an ocean.

163
00:09:05,528 --> 00:09:08,658
Its water arrives through a pipeline
from a treatment plant.

164
00:09:09,163 --> 00:09:11,613
Trick number two: save it for later.

165
00:09:12,123 --> 00:09:16,003
When there's extra river water, cities pour it
into the ground and store it in the

166
00:09:16,043 --> 00:09:18,813
aquifer, like a giant
underground piggy bank.

167
00:09:19,323 --> 00:09:23,773
The city of Phoenix alone has stored more than a
hundred and eighty thousand acre-feet.

168
00:09:24,256 --> 00:09:27,086
Trick number three: swap farms for houses.

169
00:09:27,516 --> 00:09:30,766
In Arizona, farms use about seventy
percent of all the water.

170
00:09:31,096 --> 00:09:34,026
An acre of crops can drink several
feet of water a year.

171
00:09:34,416 --> 00:09:37,406
The same acre covered in homes
often uses a lot less.

172
00:09:38,033 --> 00:09:39,783
And people at home changed too.

173
00:09:40,353 --> 00:09:43,163
Green lawns gave way to gravel
and cactus yards.

174
00:09:43,573 --> 00:09:47,353
Old toilets that used three or four gallons a
flush were swapped for ones that use

175
00:09:47,393 --> 00:09:48,543
about one and a quarter.

176
00:09:49,013 --> 00:09:51,903
That's a big part of why each
person uses so much less.

177
00:09:52,594 --> 00:09:54,644
And trick number four is my favorite.

178
00:09:55,134 --> 00:09:58,524
In twenty twenty-four, the Gila
River Indian Community,

179
00:09:58,674 --> 00:10:03,204
just south of Phoenix, switched on solar
panels built right over a canal.

180
00:10:03,654 --> 00:10:06,144
The first project like this in
the United States.

181
00:10:06,776 --> 00:10:11,106
The panels make clean power, and the shade
keeps the water from evaporating.

182
00:10:11,556 --> 00:10:13,826
Electricity on top, water underneath.

183
00:10:14,216 --> 00:10:16,466
The Hohokam would probably approve.

184
00:10:17,155 --> 00:10:19,845
So, how does Phoenix exist in the desert?

185
00:10:20,255 --> 00:10:21,785
Not with one magic pipe.

186
00:10:22,115 --> 00:10:27,225
It's ancient canals, a president's dam, a river
pumped uphill across the desert,

187
00:10:27,475 --> 00:10:29,505
and a lot of very careful counting.

188
00:10:30,097 --> 00:10:33,087
It's a city that has to plan a
hundred years ahead,

189
00:10:33,237 --> 00:10:35,947
because the desert never forgets
to send the bill.

190
00:10:36,470 --> 00:10:38,420
Remember that swimming pool
from the beginning?

191
00:10:39,110 --> 00:10:42,790
Some of the water in it may have traveled almost
two hundred miles from the Colorado

192
00:10:42,830 --> 00:10:49,000
River, climbed up and across the desert, and flowed
down a canal the Hohokam might recognize.

193
00:10:49,635 --> 00:10:50,985
Now I want to hear from you.

194
00:10:51,455 --> 00:10:54,485
Would you live in a city that runs on a
three-hundred-mile canal?

195
00:10:54,935 --> 00:10:56,545
Or is that a little too exciting?

196
00:10:56,995 --> 00:10:57,965
Tell me in the comments.

197
00:10:58,455 --> 00:11:01,225
And if you want to see how other cities
pull off the impossible,

198
00:11:01,635 --> 00:11:04,485
subscribe, because we've got a lot
more of them coming.
